# Corpus: filings are never committed, their identifiers always are.
# See HYBRID-RETRIEVAL-SEC-PLAN.md §2.
backend/corpus/filings/
# Dev-set filings for the stability check, re-fetchable via
# scripts/fetch_calibration_filings.py.
backend/corpus/calibration/
# Model output for the eval corpus. Gitignored because it must not be read
# before labeling is complete, and because it is derived from the filings.
#
# Globs, not exact paths. `labels.jsonl` as an exact path let the backup
# relabel.py writes, `labels-before-relabel-<stamp>.jsonl`, sit untracked in the
# repo -- hand-label data, one `git add -A` away from being committed. Backups
# now go to corpus_paths.backup_dir(), outside the repo; these globs are the
# second line of defence, not the first.
backend/corpus/labels*.jsonl
backend/corpus/predictions*.jsonl
backend/corpus/label-backups/
backend/corpus/*.txt
backend/corpus/*.htm
backend/corpus/*.html
# The materialised chunk store: 11,621 records holding 22.9 MB of filing text.
# corpus_paths.chunks_dir() puts it beside the filings, outside the repo; this
# covers the repo-relative default, which is what a run without RAG_FILINGS_DIR
# writes. It sat untracked and unignored while the query set beside it was
# covered -- the same "untracked is not protected" hazard, one directory over.
backend/corpus/chunks/

# The 65-query retrieval set. Gold is (accession, quoted span), so the file
# holds verbatim filing text -- corpus content reaching the public repo past
# every guard aimed at corpus/filings/. It is also the input to every Phase 3
# number, which puts it in the same class as labels and predictions.
#
# corpus_paths.queries_dir() puts it beside the filings, outside the repo. This
# covers the repo-relative default, which is the case that bites when someone
# runs without RAG_FILINGS_DIR set. "Untracked" is not "protected".
backend/corpus/queries/
